{
  "markdown": "Despite the success of anti-PD-1 therapies in melanoma, a substantial proportion of patients with unresectable or metastatic disease experience primary or acquired resistance, and treatment options after progression on these agents remain limited. In this setting, objective response rates to subsequent single-agent immunotherapy are low (approximately 12%), and there is a clear unmet need for combination strategies that can restore or enhance antitumor immunity. Preclinical and early clinical data suggest that adding Drug X to PD-1 blockade may overcome resistance mechanisms and improve clinical outcomes.\n\nThis phase 2 trial is designed to evaluate whether the addition of Drug X to nivolumab improves the objective response rate compared with nivolumab alone in adults with unresectable or metastatic melanoma that has progressed on prior anti-PD-1 therapy. The expected improvement from 12% (control) to 30% (combination) represents a clinically meaningful benefit, and the randomized 1:1 design with a 52-week treatment period will provide robust evidence of efficacy and safety. Similar ongoing or recent trials have explored combination immunotherapies in melanoma, such as nivolumab with relatlimab (NCT03743766) or with hydroxychloroquine (NCT04464759), but none have specifically tested Drug X in this post-anti-PD-1 population. This trial will therefore address a critical gap by assessing a novel combination that may offer a new therapeutic option for patients with limited alternatives."
}
